{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "21",
    "case_summary": "A 25-year-old male presented with a history of cough producing bloody mucoid sputum for the past two months. He also experienced anorexia and had a history of intermittent upper respiratory tract infections over the last two years. Physical examination revealed reduced breath sounds at the right lung base. Laboratory tests showed normochromic-normocytic anemia, low serum iron levels, and an elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate (76 mm/first hour). A chest radiograph indicated a peripheral opacity in the right lung, and a computed tomography (CT) scan revealed a soft tissue density mass in the right lower lobe. No enlarged mediastinal lymph nodes were observed. Bronchoscopy results were negative. Transthoracic fine-needle aspiration under CT guidance did not yield diagnostic results. Comprehensive imaging of the bone, brain, and abdomen showed no extrapulmonary involvement. Surgical intervention was performed for diagnostic and therapeutic purposes. The patient underwent a right lower lobectomy and complete lymph node dissection via a right posterolateral thoracotomy. The mass was well-circumscribed and yellowish in color on macroscopic examination.\n\nMicroscopically the mass was composed of fibroblasts, collagen and inflammatory cells mainly of lymphocytes and plasma cells. There was no mitotic activity. No microorganisms were detected even on special stains.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
